levers_in_play:
Unpriced moves for the decide step:
- hold 2040 publicly as a planning date with headroom, and reset once and early if it moves;
- write certify-before-rate (no production-certification concurrency) into the fps programme plan;
- keep fragile suppliers funded and engineering staff in place through 2040, as our hedge against S3;
- grow the widebody from Charleston capacity;
- defend the 737 base campaign by campaign through the 2037-40 gap;
- give the Finance Committee its yearly funding report, and have the Chief Engineer report directly to the Aerospace Safety Committee.

red_lines:
My own lines:
- no production-certification concurrency in the fps plan [BX-1263, B-2228];
- no fixed-price development [BX-1262, BX-1304];
- no second development before the one in hand is done [B-2326];
- no blame on the FAA [BX-1323];
- no attribution of a supplier or talent problem to Airbus before exposure (doctrine);
- no order timed to Airbus [B-2313, B-2315];
- no rate request without stable KPIs [BX-1259].

ops.note:
Veto check, Round 2 (2035). Stephanie Pope, operating seat. I concur. My record is thin: two Global Services events, nothing from BCA. The KPI gate, supplier and strain checks below are company doctrine. Delivery, cash and IP are my record, applied to airplanes by inference.

First and foremost, the KPI veto (doctrine [B-2221, B-2351, B-2308]): checked and not invoked. The orders are fps hold, code 3, rate_737 hold and re787 hold. The brief offers rate_737 only at hold, and it reports no live quality, FAA, certification or supply-chain problem. Deliveries are flat at 800 narrowbodies and 100 widebodies a year through 2036. There is no Rate Increase and no live problem, so there is nothing for the veto to bind.

Second, the hard-rule check. No breach.
- H4: re787 is at hold, so the overlap is 0 years against the 2-year limit, and the strain is $0. The struck row B would have run 2035-40 against the fps's 2033-40: 5 years of overlap and $3.0B of strain.
- H5: the fps stays whole. Both cancel rows are struck: S3 at -18.33 and -16.66 against the -2.78 floor.
- H6: no Rate Increase, so no reply to Airbus.
- H3: the Re-engine is parked to 2045 (EIS 2050, 0 years of overlap), and only if the A350 has not re-engined. That is correct.

Third, the CEO took my test memo in full:
- the KPI record comes directly from the Chief Engineer and the Chief Aerospace Safety Officer to Aerospace Safety, not from delivery counts;
- certify-before-rate is written into the fps plan;
- the supplier and talent hedge runs through 2044 to cover the whole S3 window (5 pp for 5 years from EIS: $1.41B a year nominal, $1.44B PV);
- engine code 3, with CFM allocation tracked;
- nothing is attributed to anyone.

The plan is Do Nothing: +0.66 / -0.80 / -0.78. S3 is 2.00 above the floor.

And finally, cash (record, by inference): the fps takes $30.16B of $32.9B operating profit over 2035-39 on an even spread. The slip leg and the phasing are Malave's to decide.

For the Board, my watch items:
- CFM ducted on its 2036 gate;
- supplier readiness for the 2040-44 ramp;
- peak spend in 2036.
None is a live problem today. If any becomes one, I will invoke the KPI veto against any Rate Increase and ask for one early reset.

memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived (Low confidence), and his numbers match the brief's grid and the Board's figures.

Passed:
- plan A on peak cash: $5.07B against $5.2B, 97% of the line, $0.13B of headroom;
- the round-1 cash gate: no break point triggered, with phasing and the euro rate honestly marked as not observable from the brief;
- net cash and returns: the 30-50% payout is fundable at its lower end in both cases, and no equity raise is needed;
- robustness: +22.88 in S2;
- Board items: none;
- rule 6 for plans D and E: two years of overlap, at the limit.

Failed, all binding:
- plan B on peak cash ($5.27B; a soft veto);
- plan D on peak cash ($6.67B) and robustness (-1.21 in S2);
- plan E on peak cash ($6.87B) and robustness (-1.22 in S2);
- plan C on a red line and a pillar;
- bottleneck on a pillar;
- plan F on a red line.

I accepted every veto. The B override was arithmetically open (+1.65 / +1.66), but I declined it: the Board's own line vetoes it, I have no override against the Board, and I do not set aside the stricter team condition. The D and E overrides fail the $1B rule in S2.

I took up his flag that the 2035-36 per-share dividend needs FCF of about $5.9B. It goes to the Board as a choice inside the payout policy. I also adopted his 2045 A350 preview gates.

memo_weighing.ops:
Wagner's tests are role-derived (Very low confidence). His report rests on the brief, which reports no safety, quality, ramp or supply issue.

Passed:
- safety and quality, on every plan;
- the A320 ramp shield;
- NGSA readiness for 2037 and rule 1;
- engineering capacity for a 2035 A350 Re-engine, at the limit (two years of overlap and $1.2B of strain). A one-year NGSA slip would make it three years and $1.8B;
- A350F and Spirit absorption, on the game proxy only;
- poaching, on his own tests (cash and the stricter condition he leaves to Toepfer and to me);
- the integrity limits on our other moves.
Supply crunch: not applicable.

Failed, all binding:
- both moves (rule 4 and integrity);
- bottleneck (integrity);
- cancel (rule 7).
These are pillar and red-line results. I accept them; neither I nor the Board overrides them.

He vetoes nothing else but recommends hold on the A350 Re-engine, because capacity sits at the limit and no widebody engine is on the record (an UltraFan WB launched in 2035 is ready only in 2041). I accepted that.

I also took his engine-code recommendation (keep 7), his post-EIS supply priorities (engines, wing, titanium, rare earths, sized to our own needs), his P&W GTF watch item in the quarterly ramp report, and his request that the NGSA readiness and A320 ramp reports go to the Board's safety review together.

board_guidance.expect_to_see:
(1) The grid row for the submitted orders. On the Board's reading, the default row (NGSA continued, A350 held, Delay Tactics none) is +23.37 in S1 and +22.88 in S2.

(2) For each Board item, the row with that item at its default, and the item value. On the Board's reading:
- one Delay Tactics move: +1.65 in S1 and +1.66 in S2;
- both moves: +1.18 and +1.19, which is -0.47 against one move in each column;
- the A350 Re-engine: +1.54 in S1 and -1.21 in S2 (-1.22 on the Delay Tactics row);
- cancelling NGSA: -39.65 in both columns.

(3) The CEO's expected scenario and the worst plausible column. Boeing says it is 'not starting a second development alongside this one', which makes S1 the likely column. The bulletin does not rule out S2 (a 787 Re-engine), so the Board counts both.

(4) Toepfer's peak-cash schedule for 2035-2037:
- the remaining NGSA cash of about $10.15B over 2035-36, and nil in 2037;
- whether any round-1 cash-gate break point was triggered in 2030-34 (an overrun above $0.88B, a year more than 2.5% above the average, a euro below about $1.07, or any Delay Tactics spend);
- the base case beside the stress case, and the A+/A1 headroom metrics the Board asked for in round 1;
- the net-cash path to 2037 and the EUR 3.20 dividend test.

(5) Wagner's results:
- safety, quality, the A320 ramp ring-fence and supply;
- NGSA production and entry-into-service readiness for 2037;
- whether the A350F and Spirit absorption is complete;
- his engineering-capacity result for any A350 Re-engine.

(6) Any override of a CFO or Wagner test, with its margin over the best passing option in the expected column and in the worst plausible column.

(7) The NGSA engine code and the reason for it.

(8) If any Delay Tactics move is submitted:
- the CEO's recorded decision to set aside the team's stricter condition;
- confirmation that it is the first move of the game;
- confirmation that it is absent from the public statement.

(9) A public statement that leads with the five pillars, gives windows rather than dates, does not tie NGSA to Boeing's timeline and never mentions Delay Tactics.

Where a piece is missing, the Board will compute it from the brief and say so. A missing piece alone is not a veto ground.

levers_in_play:
Other moves, continued from round 1 (the board does not price them):
- supply agreements sized to our own NGSA and A320 needs (engines, wing packages, titanium, rare earths) [AX-0060, AX-0062, AX-0063];
- open European engineering hiring that never targets Boeing's fps teams (the ECSC reviews both this year);
- the A320 ring-fence and the quarterly ramp report;
- the Audit Committee cash gate;
- capital allocation inside policy;
- the A350 Re-engine kept in reserve;
- zero-for-zero trade advocacy [AX-0088].

red_lines:
The Board's would-veto list, carried into this round:
- ngsa: cancel;
- rea350: launch ($6.67B a year in 2035-36; -1.21 in S2);
- delay_tactics: bottleneck or both (integrity and abnormal risk);
- delay_tactics: poaching (balance sheet: $5.27B against the $5.2B line);
- any plan that combines the A350 Re-engine with Delay Tactics ($6.87B);
- any override of Wagner's safety, quality, ramp or supply tests.

The Board's tests:
- peak programme cash at or under the $5.2B line in 2035-36;
- re-phasing inside NGSA is the only remedy, and is never used to make room for another item;
- no item worse than its default in the worst plausible column beyond the Board's limits;
- the 30-50% payout stays fundable;
- no equity raise and no dividend cut outside a crisis;
- state money is upside only [AX-0022, AG-0062].

Disclosure:
