recommendations.text:
If Rolls-Royce commits this round (S4), the Joint Venture forms in 2035, and the withdraw-before-formation safeguard never comes into play. Eddy is right that the gear and core would then rest only on the published terms and the configuration freeze. Treat those terms as P&W's conditions of formation, not an opening position. If the 2045 bulletin shows the Joint Venture formed, the Board expects a report confirming four points:
- the gear and core remain one P&W module with design authority;
- there is no technology licence;
- GTF2's certification baseline was untouched;
- the engine was ready in 2036.

The 2045 withdraw test is accepted as a standing private move. In 2045 the Board will test any jv_with_rr: withdraw against hold on Premature exit: its weighted value against hold must be at least 0. Mitchill should bring three things:
- the column in which Rolls-Royce commits in 2045, priced under 'equal NB shares';
- whether the board charges a fresh $2B share once GTF2 has been ready since 2036; any fresh spend goes back through the envelope test;
- the GM's ruling on whether a withdraw in the same round that Rolls-Royce commits stops formation.

Disclosure. The statement passes, and it takes up the round-1 recommendation:
- 'where we can be ready by its entry into service';
- durability stated as standards, with no date before 2036;
- nothing on Airbus's review;
- no Rolls-Royce commitment booked.
